{"id": "xuk", "name": "Cairngorms National Park", "short": "Cairngorms", "place": "Scotland, United Kingdom", "lat": 57.1, "lon": -3.7, "side": "r", "cont": "Europe", "country": "United Kingdom", "kind": "Polar & mountain", "park": true, "blurb": "Pinewood, moor and arctic plateau where deer, hares and an eagle share the hills.", "tip": {"id": "reddeer", "title": "Thin out the red deer", "text": "Set Red deer to -70% and watch pine, heather and bilberry recover, hares and grouse find more food, and foxes, eagles and crows lose winter carrion."}, "sources": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Cairngorms National Park", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cairngorms_National_Park"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Caledonian Forest", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caledonian_Forest"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Mountain hare", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_hare"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Golden eagle", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_eagle"}], "notes": "The species list comes from GBIF occurrence records inside a circle around the park, so it can include nearby areas. Feeding links come from GloBI records and the sources listed where they state them; others are general ecology and are illustrative.", "imgwiki": "Cairngorms National Park", "icon": "whitebark", "tier": "overview", "nodes": [{"id": "sun", "sp": "sunlight", "xh": 0.08, "desc": "Short, dark winters and bright summers set the growing season for plants from the glens to the high plateaux."}, {"id": "rain", "sp": "rain and cloud", "xh": 0.28, "desc": "Moist, cool Highland weather keeps the moorland, bog and forest floor damp and supports mosses, heather and blaeberry."}, {"id": "snow", "sp": "snow cover", "xh": 0.5, "desc": "Long-lying snow patches persist on the high plateaux, shaping which plants and animals can live in the arctic-alpine zone."}, {"id": "peat", "sp": "peaty acid soil", "xh": 0.7}, {"id": "decomp", "sp": "decomposer community", "xh": 0.9, "name": "Fungi and soil microbes", "desc": "Break down dead pine needles, heather, and animal remains, returning nutrients to the peaty soil."}, {"id": "pine", "sp": "pinus sylvestris", "xh": 0.1, "desc": "The dominant native conifer of the Caledonian pinewood. Seedlings are often eaten by deer, which limits regrowth in many places."}, {"id": "heather", "sp": "calluna vulgaris", "xh": 0.3, "desc": "Dwarf shrub that blankets the moorland. A key winter food for mountain hares, and eaten by deer and grouse."}, {"id": "bilberry", "sp": "vaccinium myrtillus", "xh": 0.5, "wiki": "Bilberry", "desc": "Low shrub of the pinewood floor and moor; its leaves and berries feed deer, hares and capercaillie."}, {"id": "rowan", "sp": "sorbus aucuparia", "xh": 0.7, "wiki": "Rowan", "desc": "Small native tree of glens and woodland edges that bears berries and leaves taken by birds and mammals.", "ic": "aspen"}, {"id": "crowberry", "sp": "empetrum nigrum", "xh": 0.9, "name": "Crowberry", "desc": "Evergreen dwarf shrub of moor and mountain ground whose berries are eaten by grouse and capercaillie."}, {"id": "reddeer", "sp": "cervus elaphus", "xh": 0.06, "name": "Red deer", "wiki": "Red deer", "desc": "Scotland's largest land mammal. Heavy grazing and browsing on heather, bilberry and young pines shapes how forest and moor regrow.", "ic": "elk", "badge": "Keystone grazer"}, {"id": "roedeer", "sp": "capreolus capreolus", "xh": 0.2, "name": "Roe deer", "desc": "Smaller deer of woodland edges that browses shrubs, heather and bilberry, and young trees."}, {"id": "hare", "sp": "lepus timidus", "xh": 0.34, "desc": "Isolated Scottish population of the moors and hills; eats heather and, in snowy times, twigs and bark. A main prey of golden eagle and fox."}, {"id": "caper", "sp": "tetrao urogallus", "xh": 0.5}, {"id": "grouse", "sp": "lagopus lagopus", "xh": 0.66, "name": "Red grouse", "wiki": "Red grouse", "desc": "Moorland gamebird that feeds mainly on heather shoots and also on crowberry and cowberry; prey for eagles and foxes."}, {"id":